  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 28.417,
   "duration": 12.515,
   "text": "Now count the odd numbers in each sum. Three of them give three times three. Call that count n. For one plus three plus five, n is three. So it seems that the first n odd numbers add up to n times n.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "count": 30.776,
    "rule": 37.157
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 30.767,
   "mark": "count",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 37.15,
   "mark": "rule",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover_end",
   "t": 40.917,
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 40.917,
   "duration": 10.595,
   "text": "But why should odd numbers make squares at all? Five examples show a pattern. They do not prove it for every n. The question mark means: not proved yet. We need a reason.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "q": 43.558,
    "qm": 47.626
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 43.55,
   "mark": "q",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "claim",
   "t": 43.55,
   "id": "misconception-examples",
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 47.617,
   "mark": "qm",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover_end",
   "t": 51.5,
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 53.2,
   "duration": 17.528,
   "text": "A square number is a square of tiles. Nine tiles make three rows of three: a three by three square, with side three. Each sum adds one more odd number. So ask: what does one more odd number add to a square of tiles? Start with the first odd number, one: one tile, a square with side one.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "nine": 55.412,
    "ask": 60.366,
    "t": 66.489
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 60.35,
   "mark": "ask",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 66.483,
   "mark": "t",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover_end",
   "t": 70.717,
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 70.717,
   "duration": 20.246,
   "text": "Add the next odd number, three. Three tiles go around it: to the left, below, and in the corner. Together they make an L shape: a column and a row that share a corner. The square now has side two. One plus three is four tiles, the same four as in the sums. Each L has the color of its odd number, and its number lights up in the sums.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "l2": 72.628,
    "four": 82.72,
    "color": 86.779
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 72.628,
   "mark": "l2",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 82.712,
   "mark": "four",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 86.778,
   "mark": "color",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover_end",
   "t": 90.962,
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 90.962,
   "duration": 17.699,
   "text": "The next L has five tiles, and makes side three. Seven tiles make side four. Nine tiles make side five. Each L adds one to the side. So after n Ls, the side is n. Here n counts the Ls, and each L is one odd number, so it is the same n as before.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "g3": 92.723,
    "g4": 94.307,
    "g5": 96.464
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 92.712,
   "mark": "g3",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 94.295,
   "mark": "g4",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 96.462,
   "mark": "g5",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover_end",
   "t": 108.645,
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 109.845,
   "duration": 10.997,
   "text": "Why is each L the next odd number? Look at the L around the four by four square. It has a row of four, a column of four, and one corner. Four plus four plus one is nine.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "old": 112.052,
    "parts": 114.754,
    "col": 116.212,
    "corner": 117.46
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 112.045,
   "mark": "old",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 114.745,
   "mark": "parts",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "claim",
   "t": 114.745,
   "id": "mechanism-l",
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 116.212,
   "mark": "col",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 117.445,
   "mark": "corner",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover_end",
   "t": 120.828,
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 122.028,
   "duration": 26.924,
   "text": "The next L goes around the five by five square, and makes six by six. It is drawn faint, because we have not added it yet. It has five plus five plus one: eleven. Each L is two bigger than the one before: one more in its row, and one more in its column. The first L is the first tile: no row, no column, only its corner. It is one. So the Ls are one, three, five, seven, and so on: the odd numbers, in order.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "next": 124.674,
    "two": 132.661,
    "odd": 138.335
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 124.662,
   "mark": "next",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "bookmark",
   "t": 132.645,
   "mark": "two",
   "issues": [],
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "claim",
