  },
  {
   "kind": "voiceover",
   "t": 212.428,
   "duration": 37.702,
   "text": "One more thing. A sum of the first ten odd numbers stopped at nineteen. Where does a sum of n odd numbers stop? The L that makes side n tells us. It goes around a square of side n minus one. Here n is five, and n minus one is four. So it has n minus one, plus n minus one, plus one tiles. The brackets keep each n minus one together. Two copies of n make two n, which means two times n. Take away the two ones, and add the corner: two n minus one. Check: n equals five gives nine. N equals ten gives nineteen, where the sum stopped. N equals one gives one, the first tile.",
   "bookmarks": {
    "n": 224.421,
    "sym": 227.323,
    "l2": 234.303,
    "l3": 240.466,
    "check": 242.065
   },
   "scene": "OddSquares"
  },
  {
